{#- Simulated bulb and plug firmware for the hallway circuit.

    While a device has power it follows its last command. When power comes back after the last command,
    it applies its power on behavior the way Zigbee2MQTT exposes power_on_behavior: off, on, toggle or
    previous. Changing the behavior later does not change the device until the next power cut, just as
    real firmware only reads it at power up.

    Doing this in an automation would stamp every restore with automation context. Real bulbs
    report the restore with no context at all, so the firmware lives in the template itself: the restore
    reaches the devices through binary_sensor.hallway_mains_power, whose time pattern trigger gives it a
    fresh context with no user, service or automation behind it.

    command:  input_text entity holding "<on|off> <unix seconds>"
    behavior: input_select entity holding the device's power on behavior
    current:  the device's own current state (this.state); unknown right after a reload, when the last
              command is the best guess
-#}
{%- macro device_on(command, behavior, current) -%}
{%- set parts = (states(command) ~ ' 0').split(' ') -%}
{%- set last_on = parts[0] == 'on' -%}
{%- set commanded_at = parts[1] | float(0) -%}
{%- set on_since = state_attr('binary_sensor.hallway_mains_power', 'on_since') | float(0) -%}
{%- set mode = states(behavior) -%}
{%- set mode_since = as_timestamp(states[behavior].last_changed, 0) -%}
{%- if commanded_at >= on_since -%}{{ 'true' if last_on else 'false' }}
{%- elif mode_since > on_since -%}{{ ('true' if current == 'on' else 'false') if current in ['on', 'off'] else ('true' if last_on else 'false') }}
{%- elif mode == 'on' -%}true
{%- elif mode == 'off' -%}false
{%- elif mode == 'toggle' -%}{{ 'false' if last_on else 'true' }}
{%- else -%}{{ 'true' if last_on else 'false' }}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endmacro -%}
